        "geology": "Cambrian siltstones to the north and grits and greywackes of the Cambrian Rhinog formation to the south.",
        "hydrology": "The lake consists of two distinct basins; the southern section comprises a shallow limb separated from the deeper northern section by an old and broken stone causeway. The maximum depth of 11 m occurs in the northern basin in a localised hollow. Discrete drainage to the lake is by three small inflows and it is drained to the south-east by a small stream which flows to the Mawddach estuary. At some stage in the past a wooden weir (now derelict) was constructed at the outflow, presumably to raise the level of the southern section of the lake.",
        "soils": "The north and east of the catchment is dominated by amorphous blanket peats, whereas acid ranker soils characterise the south and west.",
